// Book One, Chapter 13 (session 13). The race north to Collesanto: three nights on the road, a morning in the village, a day of getting ready.
// One night phase of three actions (the road), then a day phase of three (the hill). The night is not closed here: no rest() until chapter 14.
@chapter b1c13
@title The Road to the Hill
@book burning-city
@version 1

# start
## The Via Salaria
$ do phase("night", 3)
$ b1.company = "alone"
$ b1.hill_warned = "none"
$ b1.column = "unknown"
$ b1.hill_ready = "none"
$ b1.aelia_talk = "none"
$ _lead = 0
$ _lamps = 0
$ _saw = 0
$ _word = 0
$ _prep = 0
$ _watch = 0
$ do discover("collesanto")
You do not go at once. That is the thing that you will be ashamed of, afterward, a little, though it is also the thing that keeps you alive: you stand for the length of a breath in the street under the Caelian with the runner's wax in your hand and the whole of Rome going on around you, the ash, the carts, the women with their bundles, and you make yourself think.
It is a hundred and some miles to the Sabine hills by the Via Salaria. A man on foot, with a lame leg and twenty behind him, in grey cloaks, who cannot go by day without being asked what they are, takes six nights, perhaps seven. A man who does not tire, and does not care what he is asked, and is not afraid of the dark between the milestones, might do it in three. A vampire who runs flat out will go a good deal faster than that, and will arrive starving, with nothing in him, at the foot of a hill where he is going to be needed.
?if band("hunger") == "starving"
  You are starving already. It is not a thing that you can pretend about. You have been awake for the whole of a night of war, and you have fed on whatever came to hand, and it has been a long while since that was enough.
?end
It is the one decision that cannot be put off. You will not be going alone, or you will.
-> company

# company
## Who goes with you
:company
* Go alone, and fast
  $ b1.company = "alone"
  $ _lead += 1
  There is a sort of cleanness in it. You will be quicker alone, and nobody will have to be told what you are, and nobody will have to be waited for, and nobody will have to be kept from the thing that is done to a camp in the dark when a vampire is hungry and the people in it are the enemy. You put the wax in your belt. You find that you are quite calm.
  -> road
* Go with the captain and her hunters ?if b1.cell == "theron" or b1.war_terms == "total" or bond.theron >= 3
  $ b1.company = "hunters"
  $ bond.theron += 1
  The captain with the scarred jaw is standing at the end of the street, as though she had known where you would be. She has four of hers behind her, in plain cloaks, with their hoods up, and she does not ask. She says that the General's line has been looking for the lamp seller for a good deal longer than you have. She says that it is her own wish to be there. She says, with a small dry twist of the mouth, that it will be very much quicker if she does not have to argue with you about the terms.
  You do not argue. It is not quite agreement.
  -> road
* Go with Aulus, who knows the sect's ways ?if b1.boy == "vigiles"
  $ b1.company = "aulus"
  $ bond.rufus += 1
  Rufus brings him to the gate of the Aventine station in the grey of the morning, with a bundle and a good cloak and a borrowed mule. Aulus is pale and steady. He says that he knows how they will go, because he has gone that way himself, in the spring, with a lamp, to a place that he did not understand. He says that he will be a day behind you on the road, because he is a man and has a mule. He says that he would like to be there.
  Rufus does not say anything. He puts out his hand, and you take it.
  -> road
* Go with whom you can find ?if b1.cell == "house" or b1.coven == "stayed"
  $ b1.company = "alone"
  $ _lead += 1
  There are people who would come. You think of them, one by one, at the corner of a street, and you find that each of them has a reason that is better than the reason that you would have given them. Hanno has a ledger. Prisca has a house to hold. Tiro is not there to be asked. You go alone, in the end, with the name of each of them in your pocket, like a coin that you have not yet been told the value of.
  -> road

# road
## Three nights
:road
?if actions_left() == 3
  The gates of the city are behind you, and the Salaria runs out ahead, white under the moon, between the tombs. It is a road that is used to being walked on by people who are in a hurry. It does not care.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  The second night. The hills have begun. You have the Tiber to your right, and the road is a long pale ribbon in the dark, and there are lights in the farmhouses that you pass, small and kind and quite unaware.
?else
  The third night, and the last. The Sabine hills stand up ahead of you in a blue line, with the first thin edge of the dawn behind them, and you are a good deal nearer than you had any right to be.
?end
* Run the Via Salaria flat out, and ask at every milestone what has gone by [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> run
  $ do act()
* Go round by the river, to the ferry at Eretum, where there is an inn [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> ferry
  $ do act()
* Cut over the hills by the shepherds' track, where no road goes [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> track
  $ do act()
* Send word ahead by Collis's couriers [once] ?if actions_left() > 0 and wealth > 5
  ~> courier
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> dawn
?end
-> :road

# run
## The milestones
You run. It is an odd thing to say about it, after so long, that it is a pleasure: the long clean stride of a body that has been made to do things that bodies are not made to do, the air in the face, the whole dark country slipping past as a river slips past a boat. The first hour is joy, and the second is the beginning of the hunger, and by the third you are counting the stones.
There is a good deal to learn from them. There are the carters' inns, which are shut, and the shepherds' fires on the slopes, which are not. You stop at each. You are, in the dark, a tall figure out of the road with a cloak and a question, and it takes a certain patience to be asked nothing in return.
A shepherd at the seventh milestone has seen them go by. He says it in the voice of a man who has been told that he is mad. It was three nights ago, at the second watch. There was a lame man at the head of a column, in grey, with a clay lamp, and he thanked the shepherd for a drink of water and gave him a copper coin. He says that he counted them for the sheer foolishness of it. He says that there were twenty two.
?if b1.company == "aulus"
  Aulus is not with you. You know what he would have said. You say it to yourself, in his voice, on the road: that twenty two is the number of a cell and a half, that the sect never sent fewer than twenty to a place that it meant to finish, and that a column that size is carrying nothing but jars.
?end
$ _saw = 1
$ _lead += 1
$ do power("minor")
:the_run
* Take the shepherd's ewe, which is lame, and old, and nearly his only one, and pay him double for it
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ do earn(-2)
  $ humanity += 1
  He does not ask what you want it for. He is, very likely, past asking. You do it in the dark behind the fold with your hand on the animal's eye, and it is quick and not a thing to dwell upon, and you leave on the stone by the gate a good deal more than the ewe was worth.
* Go on hungry. There will be something later
  $ _lead += 1
  You thank him, and you go on, and you keep to the road. It is a decision, and you can feel the weight of it, like a coin in the gut.
* Ask him, instead, what he thinks that they were going to do
  $ humanity += 1
  He looks at you for a long moment. "They were going to a place that they thought they knew," he says. "I could tell by the way they walked. People walk like that when they are going to a house where they have already decided what is going to happen." He scratches under his hat. "I should have said something. I thought, it is not my road."
-> :the_run_end
:the_run_end
<~

# ferry
## The ferry at Eretum
The ferry at Eretum is a flat black barge on a rope, in a bend of the river, with a lamp at each end and a fat sleeping boatman, and an inn on the bank above it that has a thatch roof and a very low door. There are two cloaks hanging on pegs in the porch. They are grey.
You stand a long time in the dark, with the river talking to itself behind you. You can hear them in there: two men, one asleep and one awake, a long way from anyone. The awake one is saying something over and over in the old Latin, in a low flat voice, like a boy at his lessons.
They are stragglers. They have been left behind by the column with a fever, or a sore foot, or a word that was said, and they are waiting for the ferryman to open at dawn. They are not a thing that anyone would be afraid of. They are two young men in an inn with a pair of clay lamps on the table, and the lamps are alight, and the white flame in them does not flicker.
:the_inn
* Go in, and ask them where the column was going
  ~> inn_ask
  -> :the_inn_end
* Wait until they sleep, and take the lamps, and the cloaks, and go
  $ _lamps += 1
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  You wait. It takes the best part of an hour. You do it in the end with a great deal of care, in the dark, and you do not wake either of them. You carry the lamps out onto the bank with your sleeves over your hands, because you can feel the flame in them across the whole of the room, and you pour out the oil into the river, a little at a time, and watch it spread on the black water in a thin rainbow skin. You take their cloaks. You will not need them. It is a thing for the sake of doing it.
  $ _lead += 1
  -> :the_inn_end
* Do what the war says
  $ do kill("human")
  $ _lamps += 1
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ do feed("human_kill")
  $ do witness(1)
  It is not a fight. It is the worst thing about it, that it is not a fight at all. The awake one gets as far as the table, with a small bright flame in his hand, and then he does not. You do it very quickly. You tell yourself, in the cold porch afterward, with the blood in you and the river going by, that it was exactly what it was said in the quarry that it would be. You are not wrong. It makes no difference at all.
  -> :the_inn_end
:the_inn_end
<~

# inn_ask
$ do fight("human", 1)
You go in. It is a low room with a table and a smell of lamp oil and onions and the awake one stands up so fast that the bench goes over. He has a knife, and a flame, and a look on his face that you have seen before, on boys at the wharf, on a roof, in a barn: not hatred, a sort of appalled readiness. The sleeper does not wake.
:inn_exchange
* Tell him that you do not mean to hurt him, and that you want one answer
  $ do strike("talk")
* Take the knife out of his hand
  $ do strike("brutal")
* Put out the lamp with your fingers
  $ do strike("power")
?if not fight_over()
  ?if last_strike() == "hit"
    He is thrown back against the wall and the bench rolls under his feet.
  ?else
    The flame comes up across the table, and you feel it, a quick hot line across your arm.
  ?end
  -> :inn_exchange
?end
?if fight_result() == "talked"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ _lead += 1
  He puts the lamp down. He is crying, a little, without any fuss. He tells you in a low voice that they were going to a hill in the oaks, to a village, to finish something that was begun there two summers ago. He says that the master has said that this time the house above it will be done properly, because the master has found out that it was not done the first time. He says that the master says that the thing in the house is not dead.
  $ _saw = 1
?elif fight_result() == "won"
  $ _lead += 1
  $ _lamps += 1
  He is not hurt more than he has to be. You hold him against the wall with the lamp at arm's length from you, in a corner, and he tells you, in a stream, with his teeth chattering, what he has been told. It is a hill, in the oaks. It is a village. It is the house above the village. He says that the master has been saying for a month that it was not finished.
  $ _saw = 1
?else
  $ injury += 5
  $ _lead -= 1
  The lamp catches your sleeve and for a moment you are a thing that is on fire. You put it out against the floor. By the time you have done it he is gone, through the low door and into the dark, and the barge is moving on its rope. You hear the sleeper wake, and shout. You stand in the inn in the dark, burned, with a smell of cloth and skin, and what you have learned is that they are not afraid of you at all.
?end
<~

# track
## The shepherds' track
There is a way over the hills that no cart goes by. It is a goat's way, a white thread up the scree and across the high pasture and down again through the oaks, and the shepherds use it in the summer, and it saves a night and a half.
You take it. The moon is down behind the ridge and the dark is thick, and you find that you have a use for the dark that you did not know you had. You go through the oak wood as a thing goes through water, without a sound, from shadow to shadow, with the leaves moving over you. It is not a thing that you were taught. It is not a thing that you did before the cup. It is as though some part of you has been waiting a long time to be asked.
$ do power("minor")
$ _lead += 2
$ cunning_brutal -= 1
You come out above a high pasture at the end of the second hour, with the whole of the Sabine country below you in the first grey, and a fire burning in a hollow. It is a very small fire. It is a camp, with no tents, and men asleep in a ring with their hoods over their faces, and one awake by the flame with a long clay lamp across his knees. He does not see you. You are above him, in the oaks, and the wind is at your back.
There are fourteen. You count them. There ought to be twenty two.
$ _saw = 1
:the_hollow
* Go down, and put out the lamps before they wake
  ~> hollow_fight
  -> :the_hollow_end
* Go round them, and keep the lead you have
  $ _lead += 1
  It costs a long hour to go round. You do it through the brambles on the north slope, with your teeth set, with the wind carrying their smell. You will think about it later. You will think that you could have stopped them, and that you did not, and that there was a reason. It is not a very good reason. It is only that there is a village a few miles ahead that does not know, and that you cannot stay for the ones who are behind you.
  -> :the_hollow_end
* Wake the one on watch, and tell him to take them home
  $ humanity += 1
  $ b1.column = "stopped"
  $ do witness(1)
  It is a mad thing to do. You do it in a quiet voice, on the edge of the firelight, with your hands open, and the man on watch turns his head and sees a figure in the oaks with a cloak and a pale face and he does not scream. He lifts the lamp. He says nothing at all. The flame is perfectly steady. After a long time he says, in the old Latin, "The master said that you would come." He says it as a man says a thing that he has been told to say. "He said that you would try to make us put them down." He shakes his head. "He said that we should be sorry for you."
  You go on. You will not forget the voice. There is nothing to do with it but carry it.
  -> :the_hollow_end
:the_hollow_end
<~

# hollow_fight
$ do fight("human", 2)
You go down through the oaks with the wind at your back, and the first lamp goes out before the man who is holding it has opened his eyes.
:hollow_exchange
* Go through them, hard, before they can light
  $ do strike("brutal")
* Move behind them in the shadow, and take the lamps from the ring
  $ do strike("power")
* Speak, once, in a voice that makes them stop
  $ do strike("talk")
?if not fight_over()
  ?if last_strike() == "hit"
    A man goes down in the bracken with the lamp under him, and the white flame goes out in the damp.
  ?else
    A jar breaks on a stone and there is a bloom of heat that makes you turn your face away.
  ?end
  -> :hollow_exchange
?end
?if fight_result() == "won" or fight_result() == "talked"
  $ _lamps += 3
  $ _lead += 1
  $ do witness(1)
  When it is done there are eleven of them left, in the grey light, on their knees, with their hands open and the lamps in the grass. You do not know how it was done. You are standing in the middle of the ring with ash on your cloak and a smell of oil. You take the jars away, one by one, and break them on the rocks, and you are very careful where you stand.
  $ _saw = 1
?else
  $ injury += 6
  $ _lead -= 1
  They put you down in the bracken in a wash of heat, and when you can see again the ring is empty, and the fire is out, and there is only a smell of burned grass and a patch of black earth where you were lying. They have gone on. You get up. It is very quiet.
?end
