# courier
## The courier
Collis keeps a stable at the Porta Collina, as a firm keeps a stable, for no reason that anyone could have given until now: a dozen fast horses, and a man who knows the roads, and a boy who can be sent. You do not go to the stable. You send for it, from the first inn that you pass, by a coin to a muleteer, and by the next evening a boy of fourteen with a flat cap and a brown mare is sitting in the courtyard saying that he is to take a letter to the Sabine country, to a woman called Domitia, in a place called Collesanto, and that he is not to be told what is in it.
You write it on a wax tablet, at a tavern table, by a lamp. It is very hard to write. You have never found anything harder.
:the_letter
* Tell her plainly what is coming, and when
  $ b1.hill_warned = "runner"
  $ _word += 2
  You say that there are men on the Via Salaria with lamps. You say that they will come at night, from the north, and fire the thatch first. You say what the signs of them are: grey cloaks, a lame man at the head of a column, a flame that does not flicker. You say to get the children into the villa on the hill. You say to take the old with them, and to bar the door, and to wet the roofs. You write, at the bottom, your name, and then you scrape it off with the edge of the stylus, and you write the one that she knew you by.
* Say only that it is a bad time, and that they should go to the villa
  $ b1.hill_warned = "runner"
  $ _word += 1
  It is not much. It is a letter from a ghost, or from a dream, or from the sort of person that a village has heard of and does not mention. You say that there is a danger. You say that it is the same one as before. You say that the villa has a cellar, and that she knows it, and that it is the only safe place that there is. You do not sign it.
* Do not write. She will not believe it
  $ b1.hill_warned = "none"
  $ _word -= 1
  You put the tablet down, blank. It is a thing that you can imagine very clearly: the courier at the door, the old woman with a hard face, the paper that will come from nowhere, from a person who is dead. You do not know that she would think of you at all. You send the boy back to the stable with a coin.
-> :the_letter_end
:the_letter_end
$ do earn(-3)
<~

# dawn
## The foot of the hill
$ do discover("collesanto")
?if _saw > 0 and b1.column == "unknown"
  $ b1.column = "counted"
?end
?if _lead >= 3
  You are in the oak wood at the foot of the hill when the sun comes up, and there is a long clear morning ahead of you, and no sign of anyone but a boy with two goats on the track.
  $ _day_start = 2
?elif _lead >= 1
  You come to the foot of the hill in the last of the dark, with the first light beginning to colour the oaks behind it, and there are lamps at the villa wall that were not there before. They are not the sect's. They are a woman's, with a hard flame and a hand on them, and you do not know why you are so glad of them.
  $ _day_start = 1
?else
  You come to the foot of the hill at sunrise. The column is nowhere that you can see. It is a quiet morning, and the smoke from the village chimneys is going straight up, and you do not know that the lead you have has been a day or an hour or nothing at all.
  $ _day_start = 0
?end
The village is as you left it, and it is not. The roofs are new in places, the black timbers of the burned row have been cleared and patched with raw pale wood, and there is a new wall of stakes round the well. It is smaller than you remember. It is the oak trees, you think, that have grown. The square has a bench in it that was not there. There are children playing a game with sticks in the road, who stop when they see you, and stare, and then run.
-> arrival

# arrival
## Collesanto
It is not Domitia who sees you first. It is a boy on the wall of the well, a lanky one of fourteen, with long arms and a stubborn mouth, who has been looking at the north road with the face of someone who has been told to watch it, and who sees a tall figure come out of the oaks in a hood in the morning sun, and does not run, and does not shout, and slides down off the wall and comes across the square very fast.
It is Quintus. He has grown. He has a knife in his belt, a small one, such as a boy carries to cut bread. He stops a pace away and looks up into your face.
?if b1.goat == "freed"
  "The goat came back," he says. It is the first thing that he says. "It came back on its own, the day after the fire. It is a good goat. It has got fat." He swallows, and his mouth goes crooked. "I always knew it was you. I said so. Nobody listens."
?elif b1.warning == "warned" or b1.fire_seen == "seen"
  "It was you," he says. "At the fire. It was you at the well, and on the stair, and the thing in the smoke that pulled the old man out. They said that it was a god, or a demon, or Sextus, drunk. I said that it was not." He looks at you with eyes that are very bright. "I said that I knew what it was. Nobody listens to me."
?else
  "You are him," says Quintus. It is not a question. "You are the one that they said was dead. I saw you on the hill. I saw you in the dark, at the wall, when I was supposed to be asleep. I did not tell." His mouth goes crooked. "Nobody listens to me, anyway."
?end
He does not ask what you are. He does not seem to want to know. He takes your sleeve, as a child does, and pulls you across the square, toward the house at the end of the row, and he says, over his shoulder, in a rush, that there is a thing that you have to see.
?if b1.hill_warned == "runner"
  "A boy came," he says. "A boy on a horse, with a letter. Domitia has been reading it for two days. She has not said a word. She has had the men bring the old hay out of the loft, and she has had the shepherds up on the ridge with horns, and she has had the whole of the north row watered, every night, from the well, like a garden, and nobody understands why." He stops. "She said that there is a thing coming, and that it is the same thing as before, and that if anyone asked, she had it from the Lord of the Hill."
  $ b1.hill_ready = "some"
?end
The door of the house is open. There is a woman standing in it, in a plain dark gown, with a ladle in her hand, with her grey hair pulled back from a face that has gone harder, and thinner, and has not forgotten anything. She does not say your name. She does not say anything. She looks at you for a very long time, with her mouth set in a line, and then she puts the ladle down on the step.
?if b1.farewell == "promise"
  "You said that you would come back," says Domitia. Her voice is steady. It is a thing that she has had a great deal of practice in. "I did not believe it. I want you to know that I did not believe it, for two years, and that I laid a place at the table every night." She stops. "Come in. You will be hungry. Felix! Aelia!"
?elif b1.farewell == "truth"
  "You told me the truth," says Domitia. "I have not told anyone. I have said that you died at the villa, and I have said that you did not, and I have let them think what they like." She looks at your hands. They are not shaking. "I thought that I would be afraid. I find that I am only angry. Come in. You will want to see them."
?else
  "You said nothing," says Domitia. "You went away in the night with the silver, and you said nothing, and I have been two years deciding whether to forgive you." She does not smile. "I find that it is a good deal easier to decide when you are standing in front of me. Come in. The children will want to see you. Felix! Aelia!"
?end
They come out of the house together, down the stair, quite slowly. They are a good deal taller. Felix has grown into a sturdy brown boy with a gap in his teeth and a scab on one knee, who stops three steps from the bottom and looks at you with a perfectly open face, as at a stranger. Aelia has her mother's mouth. She is very still. She does not say a word. She stands behind her brother with one hand on his shoulder, and her eyes do not leave your face.
$ do meet("domitia")
$ do meet("quintus")
:the_reunion
* Kneel down, and let them come to you
  $ bond.aelia += 2
  $ bond.felix += 3
  $ humanity += 1
  You kneel in the road. It is the only thing that you can think of, and you are too tired and too frightened for anything cleverer. Felix comes down the last three steps all at once and walks into your arms like a calf. He smells of hay and woodsmoke and boy. He says nothing, and he does not let go. Aelia does not move. Over his head you see her face, and it is working very hard to do nothing.
* Say their names, and nothing else
  $ bond.aelia += 3
  $ bond.felix += 1
  You say them. "Aelia," you say. "Felix." It is all you have. Aelia lets out a long breath that she seems to have been holding since the spring. "You are late," she says. It is the thing that you have been waiting to hear, and you did not know it.
* Tell them that there is not much time
  $ bond.aelia += 1
  $ standing += 1
  They look at you. It is not what they were waiting for. Aelia's mouth goes thin, and Felix looks up at his grandmother, and then at you, and then he says, politely, that he supposes you had better come in.
-> :the_reunion_end
:the_reunion_end
It is the longest morning that you have ever known. It is, in its way, a very ordinary one. There is bread. There is a bowl of something with barley in it that you cannot eat, and that Domitia sets in front of you anyway, and that you eat a little of, so as not to be asked. Felix talks the whole time. Aelia watches you.
?if b1.reveal == "aelia" or b1.fire_seen == "seen"
  She has seen you before. She does not say so, and you do not say so, and it is there on the table between you like a third bowl.
?end
The sun comes in at the door. It is a clear bright day, high summer, and it falls across the floor in a long bar, and across your hands. It does not burn. It is a weight, and a fever, a thing that you carry as one carries a coat in a hot country, and the children, if they notice, do not mention it.
-> day

# day
## The day before
$ do phase("day", 3)
$ _prep = 0
$ _watch = 0
?if _day_start >= 2
  You have a clear day. You have a morning, and an afternoon, and a dusk, and it is not nothing.
?elif _day_start == 1
  You have a day. It is not a long one. You can already feel the evening in it, like a draught under a door.
?else
  You have the day. It is all that you have. You know it, and so does Domitia, who has been watching you across the room with her mouth set for the better part of an hour, and who now says, in a very flat voice, "Tell me what to do."
?end
Domitia puts it plainly, at the table, with her ladle in her hand. She says that she does not want to know what you are. She says that she has had two years of not wanting to, and a fire, and she has found that it makes very little difference to the thatch. She says that there will be men coming, with lamps, from the north, tonight or the next. She says that you have until the dusk. She says that the whole of the village is in the house, or will be, and that it is for you to say what they are to do.
There are four things that you could do, and a day in which to do three. It is a great deal less than a village.
:day_hub
?if actions_left() == 3
  It is early. The morning is still cool in the shade of the walls, and the dew is on the thatch, and you can hear a cock somewhere in the lower village, announcing a fact.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  It is the middle of the day. The sun is overhead, and the stones are hot, and the village has the stillness of a place in which everybody is working very hard and no one is talking.
?else
  It is the afternoon, and the light is beginning to go long and yellow across the oak woods. It is the best part of the day. You have the feeling of a man who has been given a coin and is spending it as it falls.
?end
