# thatch
## The north row
Sextus is exactly as you remember him, except older. He has the same bad knee and the same good ear. He is standing at the end of the north row with a hay hook and a crowd of twenty men and boys, and he is saying to them, in a loud flat country voice, that if it comes to be a matter of burning a man's roof or pulling it down, he would sooner pull it down himself and have the beams to build with.
?if b1.first_blood == "forced"
  He sees you. He puts down the hook, quite deliberately, and the men behind him go very quiet. He has an old man's long steady look. He says, "You were on the hill." You say that you were. He nods slowly. "I told them that I saw a ghost, and they said I had been at the wine." He picks up the hook. "I was not at the wine."
  $ bond.sextus += 2
?else
  He gives you a nod, as a man nods to a neighbour that he has not seen in a month. It is a thing that is done in this village. It is a great deal more than it looks.
?end
It is hard, plain, dirty work, and you are, God help you, rather good at it. You can lift a roof beam as a man lifts a stick. You can climb to the ridge of a thatch without a ladder. You can run along the top of a wall that a man would have to creep along. You do it for most of the morning, in the sun, with the sweat of fever on you and the whole of the village watching, and by noon the north row is a row of bare black rafters under a wet brown crust, and the old men are standing in the road, with their arms folded, quite silent.
$ _prep += 2
$ do witness(2)
$ do send_home(8)
:the_thatch
* Bring up the water from the well, and keep on bringing it, until the cisterns are full
  $ _prep += 1
  $ standing += 1
  $ do power("minor")
  You do it yourself, with two great yokes and four buckets, up and down the lane, for the best part of an hour, until the cisterns are brimming and the ground is dark with spilled water. It is a thing that nobody has been asked to see, and everybody sees. It is nothing at all to what you could do. It is the most that you will ever be allowed.
* Let the men do it, and keep your strength
  $ _watch += 1
  You stand at the corner, and give orders, and the men do as they are told, and it is done in a good deal longer. It is easier on you. It is not a thing that you will be thanked for, and you do not mind.
-> :the_thatch_end
:the_thatch_end
