# dusk
## Dusk
$ do discover("collesanto")
?if _prep >= 7
  $ b1.hill_ready = "full"
?elif _prep >= 4
  $ b1.hill_ready = "some"
?else
  $ b1.hill_ready = "none"
?end
?if must_feed()
  The light is going. You have not eaten since the sheep, if you ate then, and the hunger has come up in you slowly through the day, like a tide in a harbour, and you are aware of the heartbeat of every living thing on the hill, the dog in the yard, the hens in their house, the old woman at her fire, the two small hearts in the children's room.
  It is the sort of thing that you cannot put off. It is not a thing that you can do in the village.
  * Go down to the fold, and take a ewe, and bury what is left
    $ do feed("animal")
    $ humanity += 1
    Sextus finds you at it. He says nothing. He leans on the hurdle and watches you with his great slow eyes, and when it is done he picks up the animal and carries it away, and in the morning there is a new ewe in the fold, and no one mentions it.
  * Go to Sextus, and ask for his wrist
    $ do feed("donor")
    $ bond.sextus += 3
    $ humanity -= 1
    He thinks about it. He holds out his arm. "It is not much," he says, "but it is what I have," and you take it, and he does not look away.
  * Go on hungry, and meet them as you are
    $ _watch += 1
    $ cunning_brutal += 1
    You go on hungry. It will be a thing that you will pay for.
?end
The sun goes down behind the western oaks in a red haze, as it did on the night of the fire. The shadows come across the square. You stand at the well with your hands on the cold stone, and the whole of the village behind you, with its stripped roofs and its full cisterns and its women waiting at their doors, and the horns in the dark.
Domitia comes and stands by you. She does not say anything for a long time. She says, at last, "They will come." She says it very quietly. She says, "I find that I am not afraid. It is a very strange thing."
And on the ridge to the north, small and white and perfectly steady, in the first of the dark, there is a light.
